00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.276
But how do we make institutions more productive?

00:25:04.316 --> 00:25:12.836
That's a big challenge, right? So that's why we believe
that the AI Champions Network is our way to cross that line

00:25:12.836 --> 00:25:15.076
to enable productive organization.

00:25:16.196 --> 00:25:20.676
So after selecting the AI champions, They went through three phases.

00:25:20.716 --> 00:25:25.436
Phase one is democratization. They were given broad AI tool access.

00:25:25.476 --> 00:25:31.196
Very targeted training workshops.
And they spent a lot of time rapid experimenting.

00:25:32.156 --> 00:25:35.356
In the phase two, they go back to their team.

00:25:35.516 --> 00:25:43.756
They started working to identify the highest impact
project that would benefit not just themselves or their team,

00:25:43.796 --> 00:25:45.756
but benefit the company as a whole.

00:25:47.076 --> 00:25:56.756
And in phase three, they will be the ones who are leading
the the production, the design and build of those important projects.

00:25:56.836 --> 00:26:01.156
Measures ROI and share the learning across the organization.

00:26:03.316 --> 00:26:10.356
At the same time, we need to keep in mind that if
you want the whole company to embrace and adopt AI,

00:26:10.676 --> 00:26:13.156
we need more than the AI champions.

00:26:13.356 --> 00:26:22.316
We need everyone to be on board. That's why we created this structure
of whole year of different events for all the employees

00:26:22.316 --> 00:26:30.996
in the company with three apps AI,
upskill training sessions, three demo days open to everyone.

00:26:32.796 --> 00:26:37.156
We want to build momentum, not just running a one time initiative.

00:26:37.716 --> 00:26:41.836
And of course, a lot of this are being supported by the AWS team.

00:26:43.716 --> 00:26:49.036
Lastly, I want to talk about a few
risks that we are actively managing.

00:26:49.876 --> 00:26:58.436
So first of all, data security needs to be built in with very
strict governance, encryption and access control.

00:26:59.636 --> 00:27:07.756
Any system that touch sensitive data needs to be managed
with the same rigor as any of your other production systems.

00:27:08.996 --> 00:27:17.716
Secondly, as people get more used to working with AI over time,
they might lose their critical thinking capabilities.

00:27:18.196 --> 00:27:23.956
We need to avoid that by making sure that humans are in the loop
for all the critical checkpoints.

00:27:26.116 --> 00:27:34.796
Thirdly, we need to constantly review and retire the old
processes so that we can create AI native processes.

00:27:35.276 --> 00:27:42.276
Otherwise, people will be working on both processes,
which are worse than just working on the old processes.

00:27:43.516 --> 00:27:48.436
And lastly, once the Pandora's box is opened,

00:27:49.076 --> 00:27:56.996
everyone wants to experiment everything in their own tools,
in their own way, and deliver their own output,

00:27:57.036 --> 00:28:02.116
which might not necessarily align with everyone else.

00:28:02.436 --> 00:28:09.076
And therefore, we need to build this culture
of rapid proof of concept with clear kill switch.

00:28:09.876 --> 00:28:16.876
We need to fail on purpose with no attachment
on what we have built, but what we can impact.

00:28:17.516 --> 00:28:22.796
This is why we created the AI Champions Network
with supervision from AI leadership Group.

00:28:23.396 --> 00:28:29.756
Our goal is not to slow things down,
but to make sure the gains compound rather than cancel out each other.

00:28:31.836 --> 00:28:39.956
So lastly, I want to close my session by Re-emphasizing
the difference between individual AI and institutional AI.

00:28:40.396 --> 00:28:42.796
Individual AI creates productive people.

00:28:43.516 --> 00:28:50.036
Institutional AI creates productive organization,
and as a company, we need to build towards institutional AI.

00:28:50.476 --> 00:28:54.516
Thank you. Next, I would like to invite Derek and Fabian Beck.

